[2026-07-08 16:13:15] [DIAGNOSIS]:
The error at line 199 arises from the expression r1.submittedAt > r2.submittedAt. In Alloy, the > operator can only be used between Int values, but here r1.submittedAt and r2.submittedAt are of type Time. Since Time is modeled as a sig, you cannot compare Time atoms with > or < directly. Instead, you must use the explicit Time ordering provided by util/ordering[Time] (aliased as TO), using TO/lt or TO/gt for less-than or greater-than comparisons over Time atoms.

[FIX INTENT]:
Replace the illegal use of '>' on Time atoms with the correct Alloy idiom using the TO/gt operator from util/ordering[Time].

[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:
- Step 1: In pred R1R2R3, locate the line:
      (r1.classification = h && r2.classification = l && r1.submittedAt > r2.submittedAt) =>
- Step 2: Replace r1.submittedAt > r2.submittedAt with TO/gt[r1.submittedAt, r2.submittedAt], so that the line reads:
      (r1.classification = h && r2.classification = l && TO/gt[r1.submittedAt, r2.submittedAt]) =>

[RATIONALE]:
This fix is Alloy 6 compliant because it uses the built-in ordering module to compare Time sig atoms, as required, and is different from previous failed attempts which focused on let-bindings or parentheses rather than correcting the operator misuse.

[LESSON]:
When modeling time in Alloy using an abstract sig, always use the ordering module's relations (TO/lt, TO/gt, etc.) for comparisons, never the native numeric operators.
